---
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// PROTOTYPE ATTRACTION №1 — "When is it data?"  (design experiment, 2026-08-25)
//
// WHAT THIS IS. One isolated attraction from the proposed Data park, built so Huayin + CG2 can judge
// whether the park idea is delightful and workable in a browser. It is NOT production architecture,
// NOT a reusable design system, and NOT linked from any navigation. It is excluded from the sitemap
// in astro.config.mjs for the same reason: an experiment should not advertise itself as a surface.
//
// THE ONE DESIGN RULE I HELD. Attention type is expressed through FORM. A joke is folded because you
// choose to unfold a joke. A hard question is sealed because you choose to break a seal. A rigorous
// definition is a flat technical note because rigour does not need ornament. A demonstration is a
// physical object that CHANGES when you operate it. None of these is a card. Consistency is not the
// metric here; fidelity of each object to the kind of attention it wants is.
//
// NO-JS IS THE BASELINE, NOT THE FALLBACK. Every reveal on the main path is `<input type="radio">`
// or `<input type="checkbox">` plus a `:checked ~` sibling selector, and every fold is `<details>`.
// The entire intellectual walk — every question, every answer, the whole demonstration, the sealed
// plaque, the joke, the technical trail — works with JavaScript disabled and always has. The ONLY
// script on this page reveals and wires the reaction demo, whose controls ship `hidden`, so a reader
// without JS never meets a dead button.
//
// FIDELITY. The vocabulary at the end is the Afternoon's own, not invented for the occasion:
// a universe is "a set of points plus the law that says when a point exists"; an anchor is a
// governed partition written as its coordinates; `revenue @ {store, month}` is "a complete
// analytical name — the family, at the address". The attraction walks from a bare 42 to exactly
// that shape, which is also the mark the homepage's Data pillar already carries: `F @ A`.
//
// NO PUBLICATION FACT IS TYPED HERE. Every citation below is derived through data/publications.ts,
// so this file carries zero literal Zenodo tokens and needs no consumers.json row (G7). Do not type
// a DOI, a version, or a count into this page.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { citation } from '../../data/publications';

const theory = citation('w-theory-of-data');
const primer = citation('w-tod-primer');

// The five stored values of the demonstration. Every one of them is 42. Every one of them is typed
// as a number. Every one of them is about something completely different — which is the whole point,
// and which no amount of storage can tell you.
const COLUMN = [
  { about: "John's age", unit: 'years', note: 'and only on the day it was recorded' },
  { about: "Mary's age", unit: 'years', note: 'a different person, the same numeral' },
  { about: 'the loading-bay temperature', unit: '°C', note: 'nobody here is 42 degrees old' },
  { about: 'the bus that stops outside', unit: 'route', note: 'a name that happens to be a numeral' },
  { about: 'the answer to life, the universe, and everything', unit: '—', note: 'famously unhelpful' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="When is it data? — a prototype attraction"
  description="A short walk from a bare number to a complete analytical name. One prototype attraction from the proposed Data park — a design experiment, not a published account."
  wide={true}
  chip={false}
>

  {/* ── ENTRANCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <header class="gate" aria-labelledby="gate-h">
    <p class="gate-strip mono">The Data Park · prototype attraction №1</p>
    <h1 id="gate-h" class="gate-h">When is it data?</h1>
    <p class="gate-lede">
      A walk of about four minutes, starting from a single number and ending at the smallest serious
      thing you can say about it. You will be asked to commit to an answer before each reveal. That
      is the whole method; guessing wrong is the fastest way through.
    </p>
    <p class="gate-warn mono">
      This is a design experiment, not a published account. Nothing here is a claim the site is
      making. The rigorous treatment is at the far end, in its own clothes.
    </p>
  </header>

  {/* ── 1 · THE SPECIMEN ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="plinth-room" aria-labelledby="spec-h">
    <h2 id="spec-h" class="visually-hidden">The specimen</h2>

    <figure class="plinth">
      <div class="specimen" aria-hidden="true">42</div>
      <figcaption class="plinth-label">
        <span class="plinth-cat mono">specimen 01</span>
        <span class="plinth-name">A value</span>
        <span class="plinth-prov mono">provenance: none · about: nothing · acquired: from nowhere</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="ask">
      <p class="ask-q">Before you scroll — what is it?</p>
      <fieldset class="opts">
        <legend class="visually-hidden">What is 42?</legend>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q1" id="q1a" />
        <label class="opt" for="q1a">A number.</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">
          Correct — and that is the entire extent of it. It is a number, and it is about nothing.
          Hold on to how little that is.
        </p>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q1" id="q1b" />
        <label class="opt" for="q1b">A quantity of something.</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">
          Of <em>what</em>? Nothing on the plinth says. You supplied the “of” out of your own head,
          which is exactly the reflex this walk is about to take away from you.
        </p>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q1" id="q1c" />
        <label class="opt" for="q1c">The answer to life, the universe, and everything.</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">
          Held for later. It is a better joke than it looks, and it is a joke about precisely this.
        </p>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q1" id="q1d" />
        <label class="opt" for="q1d">Not enough to say.</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">
          Then you already have the instinct. The rest of this is just making it precise enough to
          build on.
        </p>
      </fieldset>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 2 · THE FIRST BINDING ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="beat" aria-labelledby="b2-h">
    <p class="beat-num mono">one binding later</p>
    <p class="statement">age = 42</p>
    <h2 id="b2-h" class="beat-h">Something arrived. Be precise about what.</h2>

    <div class="ask">
      <fieldset class="opts">
        <legend class="ask-q">Do you now know how old someone is?</legend>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q2" id="q2a" />
        <label class="opt" for="q2a">Yes.</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">Whose?</p>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q2" id="q2b" />
        <label class="opt" for="q2b">No.</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">
          No. What arrived is a <strong>kind</strong>, not a subject. You now know the value is an
          age, which tells you what may lawfully be done to it — 42 years, never 42 kilograms, and
          adding it to another age gives you nothing anybody wanted. That is real, and it is not
          enough.
        </p>
      </fieldset>
    </div>

    <p class="beat-out">
      A kind is the cheapest thing to add and the first thing people mistake for meaning. It rules
      out nonsense. It does not yet say what the number is <em>about</em>.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── 3 · THE SECOND BINDING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="beat" aria-labelledby="b3-h">
    <p class="beat-num mono">two bindings later</p>
    <p class="statement">John's age = 42</p>
    <h2 id="b3-h" class="beat-h">Now it is about somebody. Is it data?</h2>

    <div class="ask">
      <fieldset class="opts">
        <legend class="ask-q">Commit.</legend>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q3" id="q3a" />
        <label class="opt" for="q3a">Yes. It is about John, it has a value, it has a kind.</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">
          Then answer one more. Is it true?
          <span class="ans-turn">
            You cannot say. <strong>Age is not a property of a person.</strong> It is a property of a
            person <em>at a time</em>. This sentence was false last year and will be false next year,
            and it contains nothing that tells you which year it is claiming. It is not yet the kind
            of thing that can be checked — and a thing that cannot be checked is not yet a fact.
          </span>
        </p>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q3" id="q3b" />
        <label class="opt" for="q3b">Still missing something.</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">
          Yes — <strong>when</strong>. Age is not a property of a person; it is a property of a
          person at a time. This sentence was false last year and will be false next year, and it
          says nothing about which year it is claiming. Nothing here can be checked, so nothing here
          is yet a fact.
        </p>

        <input class="opt-in" type="radio" name="q3" id="q3c" />
        <label class="opt" for="q3c">Which John?</label>
        <p class="ans" role="note">
          Good — you found the other one. Two gaps, then: <strong>which John</strong>, and
          <strong>when</strong>. Both are the same kind of gap. Both are asking the same question:
          <em>where in the world is this number pointing?</em>
        </p>
      </fieldset>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 4 · THE DEMONSTRATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* The aha. A physical object you operate, and operating it drains the authority out of a number
      that was perfectly well-formed a moment ago. The scale change is the argument: the SUM does not
      disappear (it is still arithmetically correct) — it SHRINKS, because what it lost was standing,
      not correctness. */}
  <section class="rig" aria-labelledby="rig-h">
    <p class="rig-strip mono">the demonstration · operate the lever</p>
    <h2 id="rig-h" class="rig-h">Five stored values. One column. Add them up.</h2>
    <p class="rig-lede">
      Every value below is stored. Every value is typed as a number. Your database will add this
      column without hesitating and without warning you. Go on — the total is right there.
    </p>

    <div class="rig-body">
      <input class="lever-in" type="checkbox" id="lever" />

      <div class="stack" role="group" aria-label="A stored column of five values">
        {COLUMN.map((row, i) => (
          <div class="cell">
            <span class="cell-i mono">{`row ${i + 1}`}</span>
            <span class="cell-v">42</span>
            <span class="cell-about">
              <span class="cell-about-name">{row.about}</span>
              <span class="cell-about-unit mono">{row.unit}</span>
              <span class="cell-about-note">{row.note}</span>
            </span>
          </div>
        ))}

        <div class="total">
          <span class="cell-i mono">sum</span>
          <span class="total-v">210</span>
          <span class="total-verdict">
            Arithmetically correct. About nothing at all.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <label class="lever" for="lever">
        <span class="lever-knob" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="lever-txt">
          <span class="lever-a">Open the labels — say what each value is about</span>
          <span class="lever-b">Close the labels</span>
        </span>
      </label>
    </div>

    <p class="rig-punch">
      The computer never objected. It had nothing to object <em>with</em>.
    </p>
    <p class="rig-after">
      Storage held all five values perfectly. Storage held the type perfectly. Storage will hold the
      210 perfectly, for years, and serve it to anyone who asks. What storage never held is the one
      thing that made the sum meaningless — <strong>what each number was about</strong>. That was
      never in the column. It was in your head, and it was in nobody else's.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ── 5 · THE PLAIN NOTE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* Different clothes, deliberately: no ornament, no reveal, no interaction. The rigorous line does
      not perform. It is a flat note you could photocopy. */}
  <section class="note-room" aria-labelledby="note-h">
    <div class="tnote">
      <p class="tnote-cap mono">the smallest serious formulation</p>
      <h2 id="note-h" class="tnote-h">A datum is a typed value at an analytical point.</h2>
      <dl class="tnote-dl">
        <dt>typed</dt>
        <dd>
          what kind of thing the value is — and therefore what may lawfully be done to it. This is
          the part <code class="mono">age = 42</code> supplied.
        </dd>
        <dt>at an analytical point</dt>
        <dd>
          what the value is about: which thing, in which world, at which grain. This is the part that
          five identical 42s did not have, and the part that no storage layer can supply for you.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p class="tnote-take">
        A number is not data until you can say what it is about, and where in the world that is.
        Storage does neither.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── 6 · THE FOLDED JOKE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* A joke is folded because unfolding a joke is a choice. It is also load-bearing: Adams wrote the
      best available gag about an unanchored datum, and saying so is a teaching move, not a gag. */}
  <section class="aside-room" aria-labelledby="fold-h">
    <details class="fold" id="the-fold">
      <summary class="fold-tab">
        <span class="fold-tab-mark mono">fold out</span>
        <span class="fold-tab-txt" id="fold-h">The joke you were probably already making</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="fold-in">
        <p>
          Deep Thought computes for seven and a half million years and returns <strong>42</strong>.
          The joke is usually filed under <em>absurdism</em>. It is not. It is a joke about data.
        </p>
        <p>
          The machine's answer is correct. Nobody can use it, because nobody knows the question — and
          the question is precisely the thing that would have said what the 42 was <em>about</em>.
          Adams built a seven-and-a-half-million-year computation that produces a perfectly typed
          value at no analytical point whatsoever, and then made everyone in the scene react exactly
          the way an analyst reacts to a number in a spreadsheet with no column header.
        </p>
        <p class="fold-kicker">
          The sequel to that joke is that they then have to build a second, much larger computer to
          recover the question. Which is roughly what a data-governance project is.
        </p>
        {/* The reaction demo. Authored HERE rather than built by script, for a reason worth writing
            down: Astro scopes a component's <style> by stamping its elements at BUILD time, so
            script-created nodes get none of it and render unstyled. The first cut did build these in
            JS and they came out as raw mono text. Authoring the markup and letting the script only
            UNHIDE and wire it keeps the styles scoped AND keeps the no-JS promise — `hidden` never
            lifts without JS, so a reader without it sees the joke and no dead controls. */}
        <div class="react" data-react hidden>
          <p class="react-disc mono">
            prototype — reactions are local to this tab, stored nowhere, and reset when you reload.
            The counts below are a sample, not real visitors.
          </p>
          <div class="react-row">
            <button class="react-btn" type="button" data-react-btn="think" aria-pressed="false">
              <span class="react-glyph" aria-hidden="true">✳</span>
              <span class="react-label">made me think</span>
              <span class="react-n mono" data-react-n>3</span>
            </button>
            <button class="react-btn" type="button" data-react-btn="funny" aria-pressed="false">
              <span class="react-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☺</span>
              <span class="react-label">funny</span>
              <span class="react-n mono" data-react-n>11</span>
            </button>
          </div>
          <p class="react-sample">
            <span class="react-sample-who mono">a visitor left a note</span>
            “I have shipped a dashboard that summed a column exactly like this. It had a title and
            everything.”
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  {/* ── 7 · THE SEALED PLAQUE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* Heavy clothes for an unsettled question. This one is genuinely open, and it is labelled as
      unsettled rather than dressed up as a lesson with a hidden answer. */}
  <section class="aside-room" aria-labelledby="seal-h">
    <details class="seal">
      <summary class="seal-tab">
        <span class="seal-mark mono">sealed · a question we have not settled</span>
        <span class="seal-txt" id="seal-h">Is <code class="mono">age = 42</code>, with nobody attached, a datum with a part missing — or not a datum at all?</span>
        <span class="seal-open mono">break the seal</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="seal-in">
        <p>
          The two answers are not the same answer in different words, and choosing between them
          changes what you build.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A part missing.</strong> Then the point is an attribute — something a datum can
          have more or less of, filled in later, defaulted, inferred. Data is a container and the
          point is one of the fields. This is, roughly, what every database you have ever used
          believes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Not a datum at all.</strong> Then the point is constitutive: no point, no datum,
          the way there is no shadow without something to cast it. You cannot fill it in later
          because there is no “it” to fill in — there is only a numeral that has not yet become
          anything.
        </p>
        <p class="seal-note">
          The Theory of Data takes the second road. That is why <em>universe</em>, <em>existence</em>
          and <em>anchor</em> are load-bearing words in it rather than bookkeeping — and why a
          declared world can contain an address that lawfully exists with no bytes behind it, which
          is a sentence the first road cannot even form.
        </p>
        <p class="seal-note">
          What we have not settled is whether the first road is <em>wrong</em> or merely
          <em>cheaper</em>. Ask us in a year.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  {/* ── 8 · THE SERVICE CORRIDOR ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* The optional deeper trail. Quiet, unornamented, mono-labelled — it should read like walking
      through the door marked STAFF and finding the machinery, not like a "learn more" card. */}
  <section class="corridor" aria-labelledby="corr-h">
    <details class="corr">
      <summary class="corr-tab mono">
        <span id="corr-h">↳ the deeper trail — where “analytical point” goes if you follow it</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="corr-in">
        <p class="corr-warn mono">
          Three sentences from a longer theory. This is a gesture at the shape, not the account.
        </p>

        <div class="corr-beat">
          <p class="corr-term mono">universe</p>
          <p>
            A set of points, plus the law that says when a point exists. Orders exist by occurrence —
            an order is a point because it happened. Store-days exist by declaration — the pair
            (store 12, yesterday) is a legitimate location whether or not anything happened there.
            Two different kinds of existence, and confusing them is how a denominator drifts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="corr-beat">
          <p class="corr-term mono">anchor</p>
          <p>
            A governed partition of a universe, written as its coordinates:
            <code class="mono">{'{store, day}'}</code>, <code class="mono">{'{store, month}'}</code>.
            Coarser and finer anchors are the same territory re-tiled, which is why some rollups are
            free and others are not lawful at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="corr-beat">
          <p class="corr-term mono">the complete name</p>
          <p>
            A quantity with its law, at an anchor: <code class="mono">revenue @ {'{store, month}'}</code>.
            That is the whole shape this walk has been climbing toward — the family, at the address.
            Our bare specimen wanted to be
            <code class="mono">age @ {'{person, date}'}</code>, and everything that was missing from it
            was missing from exactly one of those two halves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="corr-exit">
          If that landed, the next thing to read is not another attraction. It is at the bottom of
          this page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  {/* ── 9 · THE EXIT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* Deliberately the calmest object on the page and the only one in the site's own voice. The park
      is teaching; this is the corpus. It should feel like stepping outside. Every fact derived. */}
  <section class="exit" aria-labelledby="exit-h">
    <hr class="exit-rule" />
    <p class="exit-strip mono">you are now leaving the park</p>
    <h2 id="exit-h" class="exit-h">The actual account</h2>
    <p class="exit-lede">
      Everything above is a teaching device. It is deliberately smaller than the theory, and it
      simplifies in places a serious reader will want undone. These do not.
    </p>

    <ul class="exit-list">
      <li>
        <a class="exit-title" href={theory.href}>{theory.label}</a>
        <span class="exit-meta mono">{theory.versionTag ? `${theory.versionTag} · ` : ''}{theory.date}</span>
        <span class="exit-blurb">The foundation itself, with the proofs.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="exit-title" href={primer.href}>{primer.label}</a>
        <span class="exit-meta mono">{primer.versionTag ? `${primer.versionTag} · ` : ''}{primer.date}</span>
        <span class="exit-blurb">The vocabulary, carefully, in dependency order.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="exit-title" href="/start-here">The Theory of Data in One Afternoon</a>
        <span class="exit-meta mono">on this site</span>
        <span class="exit-blurb">
          The whole idea in one sitting, from a cold start. A teaching artifact hosted here, not a
          deposited publication.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="exit-foot">
      The park exists because some ideas are easier to walk into than to read into. It does not
      replace the corpus and is not evidence of anything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script>
    // ── THE REACTION DEMO — prototype only, deliberately non-persistent ────────────────────────
    //
    // The brief asked for ONE local demonstration of the future park's reaction idea, so the room
    // can feel it. This is that and nothing more: nothing is stored anywhere, the counts are a
    // stated sample, and the markup says so where a reader will actually see it. Reload and it is
    // gone.
    //
    // The controls are authored in the template and shipped `hidden`; this script only reveals and
    // wires them. That keeps Astro's scoped styles (which never reach script-created nodes) AND the
    // no-JS promise: without JS the block stays hidden, so nobody meets a dead button.
    //
    // The one idea actually being tested: a reaction should change how PRESENT an object is, not
    // just increment a counter. So the fold gains a little weight as it lands. That is the park
    // mechanic in miniature — popularity as physical presence — at a scale we can demo honestly
    // without a backend, accounts, or moderation.
    const box = document.querySelector('[data-react]');
    const fold = document.getElementById('the-fold');
    if (box instanceof HTMLElement && fold instanceof HTMLElement) {
      box.hidden = false;
      const counts = { think: 3, funny: 11 };
      const mine = new Set();

      const weigh = () => {
        const total = counts.think + counts.funny;
        fold.style.setProperty('--react-weight', String(Math.max(0, Math.min(1, (total - 14) / 10))));
      };

      for (const btn of box.querySelectorAll('[data-react-btn]')) {
        if (!(btn instanceof HTMLElement)) continue;
        const key = btn.dataset.reactBtn;
        if (key !== 'think' && key !== 'funny') continue;
        const n = btn.querySelector('[data-react-n]');
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (mine.has(key)) {
            mine.delete(key);
            counts[key] -= 1;
          } else {
            mine.add(key);
            counts[key] += 1;
            btn.classList.remove('just');
            void btn.offsetWidth;   // restart the animation on a repeat press
            btn.classList.add('just');
          }
          btn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(mine.has(key)));
          if (n) n.textContent = String(counts[key]);
          weigh();
        });
      }
      weigh();
    }
  </script>

  <style>
    /* ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       PROTOTYPE STYLES. Scoped to this page on purpose. The brief said not to let the existing
       component system force visual sameness, and not to treat this as a design system — so these
       rules borrow the site's TOKENS (colour, type, rule, measure, focus) and none of its component
       classes. Tokens are the shared vocabulary; components would have been the shared costume.
       ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

    .mono { font-family: var(--font-mono); }

    /* One local rhythm, so sections breathe like rooms rather than stack like cards. */
    .gate, .plinth-room, .beat, .rig, .note-room, .aside-room, .corridor, .exit {
      width: min(100% - 3rem, 46rem);
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
    .gate { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 2rem + 5vw, 6rem) 0; }
    .plinth-room, .beat, .rig, .note-room, .aside-room, .corridor {
      padding-block: clamp(3rem, 2rem + 6vw, 7rem);
    }

    /* ── entrance ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .gate-strip {
      font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    }
    .gate-h {
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 5vw, 4.5rem);
      line-height: 1.02; letter-spacing: -0.02em; margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    }
    .gate-lede {
      font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink); max-width: 34rem; margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    }
    .gate-warn {
      font-size: 0.78rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft);
      border-left: 2px solid var(--rule-strong); padding-left: 0.9rem; max-width: 34rem; margin: 0;
    }

    /* ── the plinth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .plinth {
      margin: 0 0 clamp(2.5rem, 2rem + 3vw, 4rem);
      display: grid; justify-items: center; text-align: center;
    }
    .specimen {
      font-family: var(--font-serif);
      font-size: clamp(7rem, 4rem + 18vw, 15rem);
      line-height: 0.9; letter-spacing: -0.04em;
      /* A specimen sits ON something. One hairline is enough to say plinth. */
      padding: 0 0 clamp(1.25rem, 1rem + 2vw, 2.5rem);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink);
      min-width: min(100%, 20rem);
    }
    .plinth-label { display: grid; gap: 0.3rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
    .plinth-cat {
      font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
    }
    .plinth-name { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.3rem; }
    .plinth-prov { font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }

    /* ── the Socratic ask ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    /* CSS-only commitment. The radio is the commitment; the sibling paragraph is the consequence.
       No JS anywhere in this mechanism, by design — the argument must survive a dead network. */
    .ask { max-width: 34rem; margin-inline: auto; }
    .ask-q, .opts > legend {
      font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.4;
      margin: 0 0 1rem; padding: 0; float: none;
    }
    .opts { border: 0; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
    .opt-in {
      /* Kept in the accessibility tree and keyboard-reachable; visually replaced by its label. */
      position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; margin: -1px;
      overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap;
    }
    .opt {
      display: block; cursor: pointer;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      padding: 0.85rem 0 0.85rem 2rem; position: relative;
      font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.5;
      transition: color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
    }
    .opt::before {
      content: ''; position: absolute; left: 0.35rem; top: 1.15rem;
      width: 0.62rem; height: 0.62rem; border: 1px solid var(--ink-soft); border-radius: 50%;
    }
    .opt:hover { color: var(--ds-accent, #7a5c2e); }
    .opt-in:checked + .opt { font-weight: 600; }
    .opt-in:checked + .opt::before { background: var(--ink); border-color: var(--ink); }
    .opt-in:focus-visible + .opt {
      outline: 2px solid var(--ds-accent, #7a5c2e); outline-offset: 3px; border-radius: 2px;
    }
    .opts > .ans {
      /* Closed by default; the commitment opens it. `visibility: hidden` matters as much as the
         clip: without it a screen reader announces all four answers before the reader has committed
         to anything, which is the one thing this device exists to prevent. Clipping hides it from
         eyes only; visibility hides it from everybody, and restores for everybody. */
      max-height: 0; opacity: 0; overflow: hidden; margin: 0; visibility: hidden;
      padding-left: 2rem; font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.62; color: var(--ink-soft);
      transition: max-height 0.35s ease, opacity 0.25s ease, padding 0.25s ease;
    }
    .opt-in:checked + .opt + .ans {
      max-height: 40rem; opacity: 1; padding-top: 0.1rem; padding-bottom: 1.1rem; visibility: visible;
    }
    .ans strong { color: var(--ink); }
    .ans-turn {
      display: block; margin-top: 0.7rem; padding-top: 0.7rem;
      border-top: 1px dashed var(--rule-strong);
    }

    /* ── beats ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .beat-num {
      font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 0.9rem; text-align: center;
    }
    .statement {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1rem + 3.2vw, 2.75rem);
      text-align: center; margin: 0 0 1.75rem; letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    }
    .beat-h {
      font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.2rem + 1.4vw, 2rem);
      line-height: 1.2; text-align: center; margin: 0 0 2rem; max-width: 30rem;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
    .beat-out {
      max-width: 32rem; margin: 2rem auto 0; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.62;
      color: var(--ink-soft); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 1.1rem;
    }

    /* ── the demonstration rig ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .rig-strip {
      font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    }
    .rig-h {
      font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 1.2rem + 1.8vw, 2.25rem);
      line-height: 1.18; margin: 0 0 1rem;
    }
    .rig-lede { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.62; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 2rem; max-width: 34rem; }
    .rig-body { position: relative; }
    .lever-in {
      position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; margin: -1px;
      overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .stack { border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); }
    .cell {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 4.5rem minmax(0, 5.5rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-items: baseline; gap: 0 1rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
      padding: 0.9rem 0;
    }
    .cell-i { font-size: 0.7rem; color: var(--ink-soft); letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
    .cell-v {
      font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 1.6rem;
      transition: color 0.4s ease;
    }
    /* Closed: the labels do not exist. This is the honest state of a bare column. */
    .cell-about {
      display: grid; gap: 0.1rem;
      max-height: 0; opacity: 0; overflow: hidden; visibility: hidden;
      transition: max-height 0.45s ease, opacity 0.35s ease;
    }
    .cell-about-name { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.3; }
    .cell-about-unit { font-size: 0.7rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .cell-about-note { font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.45; }

    .total {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 4.5rem minmax(0, 5.5rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-items: baseline; gap: 0 1rem;
      border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
      padding: 1.1rem 0;
    }
    .total-v {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      /* THE SCALE CHANGE. Open the labels and the total does not vanish — it is still correct — it
         loses standing. Size is the argument: authority, not arithmetic, is what drained away. */
      font-size: 2.4rem; line-height: 1;
      transition: font-size 0.5s ease, color 0.5s ease, opacity 0.5s ease;
    }
    .total-verdict {
      font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink-soft);
      max-height: 0; opacity: 0; overflow: hidden; visibility: hidden;
      transition: max-height 0.45s ease, opacity 0.35s ease;
    }

    .lever-in:checked ~ .stack .cell-about,
    .lever-in:checked ~ .stack .total-verdict { max-height: 9rem; opacity: 1; visibility: visible; }
    .lever-in:checked ~ .stack .cell-v { color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .lever-in:checked ~ .stack .total-v {
      /* The ONE deliberate use of the below-AA ink on this page: the drained total. Its meaning is
         restated in full contrast immediately beside it ("Arithmetically correct. About nothing at
         all."), so nothing is carried by the low contrast alone — the fade IS the argument. */
      font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--ink-faint); opacity: 0.8;
    }

    .lever {
      display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.8rem;
      margin-top: 1.75rem; cursor: pointer;
      border: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: 0.6rem 1.1rem 0.6rem 0.7rem;
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      transition: background-color 0.2s ease, color 0.2s ease;
    }
    .lever:hover { background: var(--ink); color: var(--paper); }
    .lever-knob {
      width: 2.1rem; height: 1.05rem; border: 1px solid currentColor; position: relative; flex: none;
    }
    .lever-knob::after {
      content: ''; position: absolute; top: 1px; left: 1px; bottom: 1px; width: 0.85rem;
      background: currentColor; transition: transform 0.25s ease;
    }
    .lever-in:checked ~ .lever .lever-knob::after { transform: translateX(0.95rem); }
    .lever-in:focus-visible ~ .lever { outline: 2px solid var(--ds-accent, #7a5c2e); outline-offset: 3px; }
    .lever-b { display: none; }
    .lever-in:checked ~ .lever .lever-a { display: none; }
    .lever-in:checked ~ .lever .lever-b { display: inline; }

    .rig-punch {
      font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 1.1rem + 1.5vw, 2rem);
      line-height: 1.25; margin: 2.5rem 0 1.25rem; max-width: 28rem;
    }
    .rig-after { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; max-width: 34rem; }
    .rig-after strong { color: var(--ink); }

    /* ── the plain technical note ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .tnote {
      border: 1px solid var(--ink); padding: clamp(1.5rem, 1rem + 2.5vw, 2.5rem);
      background: var(--paper-raised);
    }
    .tnote-cap {
      font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 1rem;
    }
    .tnote-h {
      font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.15rem + 1.7vw, 2.1rem);
      line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    }
    .tnote-dl { margin: 0 0 1.75rem; display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
    .tnote-dl dt {
      font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink);
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); padding-bottom: 0.3rem; margin-bottom: 0.45rem;
    }
    .tnote-dl dd { margin: 0; font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .tnote-take {
      font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.45; margin: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); padding-top: 1.25rem;
    }

    /* ── the fold (a joke) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    /* Presence responds to reaction: --react-weight (0…1) is set by the prototype script only. */
    .fold {
      --react-weight: 0;
      border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
      background: var(--paper-raised);
      /* The gain is deliberately small. Presence, not a trophy. */
      box-shadow: 0 calc(2px + 6px * var(--react-weight)) calc(6px + 18px * var(--react-weight))
        rgba(28, 27, 25, calc(0.04 + 0.07 * var(--react-weight)));
      transform: scale(calc(1 + 0.012 * var(--react-weight)));
      transition: box-shadow 0.5s ease, transform 0.5s ease;
    }
    .fold-tab {
      display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.9rem; flex-wrap: wrap;
      cursor: pointer; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; list-style: none;
    }
    .fold-tab::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
    .fold-tab-mark {
      font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
      border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); padding: 0.15rem 0.45rem; flex: none;
    }
    .fold-tab-txt { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.1rem; }
    .fold[open] .fold-tab { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .fold-in { padding: 1.25rem; display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
    .fold-in p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.99rem; line-height: 1.65; }
    .fold-kicker { color: var(--ink-soft); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 1rem; }

    /* the reaction demo (script-built) */
    .react { margin-top: 0.5rem; border-top: 1px dashed var(--rule-strong); padding-top: 1rem; display: grid; gap: 0.85rem; }
    /* Specificity, not preference: `.fold-in p` above is (0,1,1) and would otherwise win over a
       bare `.react-disc` (0,1,0) and render the disclaimer at body size. It did, once. */
    .react .react-disc { font-size: 0.7rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; }
    .react-row { display: flex; gap: 0.6rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .react-btn {
      display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem;
      border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); background: var(--paper);
      padding: 0.4rem 0.75rem; cursor: pointer; font: inherit; font-size: 0.86rem;
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      transition: border-color 0.2s ease, color 0.2s ease, background-color 0.2s ease;
    }
    .react-btn:hover { border-color: var(--ink); color: var(--ink); }
    .react-btn[aria-pressed='true'] { border-color: var(--ink); color: var(--ink); background: var(--paper-raised); }
    .react-btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--ds-accent, #7a5c2e); outline-offset: 2px; }
    .react-glyph { font-size: 0.95rem; }
    .react-n { font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .react-btn[aria-pressed='true'] .react-n { color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .react-btn.just .react-glyph { animation: bump 0.4s ease; }
    @keyframes bump { 0% { transform: scale(1); } 40% { transform: scale(1.45); } 100% { transform: scale(1); } }
    .react .react-sample {
      margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft);
      border-left: 2px solid var(--rule-strong); padding-left: 0.85rem;
    }
    .react-sample-who {
      display: block; font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
    }

    /* ── the seal (an open question) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .seal { border: 2px solid var(--ink); background: var(--paper); }
    .seal-tab {
      display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; cursor: pointer;
      padding: clamp(1.1rem, 0.9rem + 1vw, 1.6rem); list-style: none;
    }
    .seal-tab::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
    .seal-mark {
      font-size: 0.66rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-soft);
    }
    .seal-txt { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 1rem + 0.8vw, 1.5rem); line-height: 1.3; }
    .seal-txt code { font-size: 0.85em; }
    .seal-open {
      justify-self: start; font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink); padding-bottom: 0.15rem;
    }
    .seal[open] .seal-open { display: none; }
    .seal[open] .seal-tab { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink); }
    .seal-in { padding: clamp(1.1rem, 0.9rem + 1vw, 1.6rem); display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
    .seal-in p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.99rem; line-height: 1.65; }
    .seal-note { color: var(--ink-soft); }

    /* ── the corridor (the deeper trail) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .corr { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .corr-tab {
      cursor: pointer; padding: 1rem 0; list-style: none;
      font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink-soft);
    }
    .corr-tab::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
    .corr-tab:hover { color: var(--ink); }
    .corr-in { padding: 0.5rem 0 1.75rem; display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; }
    .corr-warn { font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; }
    .corr-beat { display: grid; gap: 0.4rem; }
    .corr-term {
      font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink);
      margin: 0;
    }
    .corr-beat p:last-child { margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.62; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .corr-beat code { font-size: 0.85em; color: var(--ink); }
    .corr-exit { margin: 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }

    /* ── the exit (site voice) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    .exit { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 2rem + 5vw, 6rem) clamp(4rem, 3rem + 6vw, 8rem); }
    .exit-rule { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); margin: 0 0 2.5rem; }
    .exit-strip {
      font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    }
    .exit-h {
      font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.2rem + 1.4vw, 2rem);
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
    }
    .exit-lede { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.62; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0 0 2rem; max-width: 34rem; }
    .exit-list { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 2rem; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 1.4rem; }
    .exit-list li { display: grid; gap: 0.25rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 1rem; }
    .exit-title { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.12rem; color: var(--ink); }
    .exit-meta { font-size: 0.72rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .exit-blurb { font-size: 0.93rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); }
    .exit-foot {
      font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 1.1rem;
    }

    /* ── narrow ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    @media (max-width: 34rem) {
      .cell, .total { grid-template-columns: 3.2rem minmax(0, 4rem) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 0.7rem; }
      .cell-v { font-size: 1.3rem; }
      .total-v { font-size: 1.9rem; }
      .fold-tab { gap: 0.5rem; }
    }

    /* ── reduced motion ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
    /* tokens.css already kills durations globally; this removes the two TRANSFORMS as well, which a
       duration override alone would leave in place as an instant jump. */
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .fold { transform: none; }
      .react-btn.just .react-glyph { animation: none; }
      .lever-knob::after { transition: none; }
    }
  </style>

</BaseLayout>
